<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Sorrento</h1><p class="st-meta">839 CE – 1137 CE (approx.) · capital Sorrento</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Minor Byzantine-era duchy on the Sorrento peninsula, independent of Naples from the 9th century until the Normans took it in 1137.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a>, an old Roman town on the peninsula south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>, was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-naples">Byzantine Duchy of Naples</a>. In the 9th century, amid the conflicts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and Arab raiders, it gained self-government under its own dukes, like neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-amalfi">Amalfi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of the duchy, which was small and overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-amalfi">Amalfi</a>. It ruled the western part of the peninsula and nearby islands, and its economy rested on fishing, farming and coastal trade. In 1039 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV of Salerno</a> seized it and gave it to his brother Guy, and it later passed among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> and Norman lords.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a> and was absorbed into the Norman <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a> by 1137, after which it was ruled as part of the kingdom's mainland territories.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-sorrento.jpg" alt="Duchy of Sorrento" width="320" height="205"><figcaption>Image: Angelus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stati_presenti_in_Campania_intorno_all%27anno_1000_(Sorrento).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>839 CE – 1137 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sorrento</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guy of Sorrento</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ducatus Surrentinus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=988&amp;focus=duchy-of-sorrento" aria-label="Duchy of Sorrento on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5032 726 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Sorrento lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5352 982L5349 976M5352 982L5362 981M5352 982L5333 1064"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5349 976h0M5362 981h0M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5352 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5352 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sorrento. <a href="/atlas?year=988&amp;focus=duchy-of-sorrento">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
